==English==

===Etymology===
1670s, from {{uder|en|la|subtrahendus}} {{m|la|numerus||number to be subtracted}}, form of {{m|la|subtrahō||I pull out from under, I subtract}} (English {{m|en|subtract}}).<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

{{surf|en|subtract|-end|t2=patient (recipient) of action}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsʌbtɹəhɛnd/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|arithmetic}} A number or quantity to be subtracted from another.
#: {{ux|en|In the subtraction 10 &minus; 4, 4 is the '''subtrahend'''.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|minor}} {{q|obs.}}

